        @X0EThis bulletin will give you a tutorial on basic methods of trouble-
shooting. These are techniques used by Salt Air Technical Support in helping
our customers over the phone.
 
A few rules to abide by are:
 
1) @X06Keep it simple.  Alleviate any variables in the situation that you can.
   For example, If you're having trouble getting PCBoard to run, try starting
   with just 1 node instead of 5 or 10.
 
@X0E2) @X06Don't assume anything.  Example, if a command is set to security 110, and
   you assume that it's set to 10, the command won't run unless you're a sysop.
   The sysop won't find the problem, because he'll assume that that's not the
   cause and won't check it.
 
@X0E3) @X06Causes of a problem are not limited to a switch every time.  There are many
   switches for pcboard, but problems can also be caused by network errors,
   path problems, or invalid filenames/pathnames, and batch file operations.
 
@X0E4) @X06The error messages you receive are not always the *initial* cause of the
   problem, but are often a good indication of the immediate problem.  Note
   the errors as they pop up.  If they're too fast for you to read, put a
   pause in the batch file that's running, or the file pcboard is displaying,
   or whatever is necessary.
 
 
@X0BErrors:
--------
@X03
If you see an error occur, you should troubleshoot it depending on which
program is running at the time.
 
 
@X0B1) Batch Files
 
@X03If the error occurs while running a batch file, you'll first want to find out
which line is returning the error.
 
To do this, take any any @ECHO OFF or ECHO OFF statements in the batch file.
Now, put a pause every few lines or as needed so that you can see, by
screenful, every line that is executed.
 
When you run the batch file again, the error will display right under the
line at fault.  A simple strategy for finding the problem with these:
 
@X09A) Type the command from the same window in the same directory as the batch
   file.  If it runs, then the current directory (while the batch file is
   being run) is not the directory that it should be.
   If the command does NOT run, check the spelling, check to see that it
   exists, and make sure that it is the correct type of file (OS/2 file,
   Dos file, .BAT, .CMD, etc.)
 
B) If no error is displayed, then it is a program that is executing that does
   not behave as expected.
 
@X03@X0BOtherwise-
-----------
@X03If the problem is with PCBoard, there may be some numbers displayed.  The
numbers might look like this:
@X07(39:3:3:1)
@X03There should not be more than 4 number separated by colons (:).  These numbers
are relayed from the operating system, and the problem does not come from
PCBoard.  If you have a list of Dos error codes, you can look up the problem.
If not, then you (C)omment to SysOp and tell them the error code.  They can
tell you what the error indicates.
 
If the error does not include the number set, then the error is generated by
PCBoard.
 
Errors with numbers (which are generated and returned by the operating system)
*are* sometimes fixed by changing PCBoard's configuration in some way.
 
@X0BPPEs
------
@X03Some errors are generated by @X05PPE@X03s.  A ppe is a program written with the PPLC
programming language compiler.  These programs are written by other sysops or
PCBoard users.  Error messages can be returned by these programs.  In the event
that you are running a ppe that you have downloaded, written, or are even
unaware of, the errors are unfamiliar to you and Salt Air Tech Support.
 
If you suspect that an error message you see is being generated by a PPE, there
is a way that you can test it, even if you don't know what or where the PPE is.
 
@X09A) Find the batch file that you're running to launch the node in question.
   Defaults may be BOARD.BAT, NODE1.BAT (1 represents node number), or
   STARTOS2.CMD.
 
B) Load a text editor and load the batch file into the editor.  Locate the line
   that says:
@X07   SET PCB=
   @X09There may be additional parameters following this.  They AND their order is
   irrelevant.
 
C) Append to the end of this line:
   @X07/NOPPE
   @X09This will disable execution of any PPEs on all nodes that are launched with
   that batch file.
 
@X03Try to reproduce the problem.  If the error message pops up, then the it is not
caused by a ppe.  If the error does not appear, then the PPE may need to be
removed.
 
