@X0BLockups:
--------
@X02@X03Lockups can be caused by many things and it is generally very difficult to find
the cause.  A few things that often cause lockups:
 
@X05  Memory conflicts
  IRQ conflicts
  Slow processing @X08(appears locked)
  @X05
@X03Memory conflicts are often a conflict between a memory resident program (a TSR)
such as a driver that is loaded in the config.sys or autoexec.bat.  This can be
tested by rebooting and pressing F4 or F8 under DOS and Windows or ALT-F1 under
OS/2.  Then select the drivers to install.  Try this a few times until you can
narrow down the program that is causing the conflict.
 
IRQ conflicts are hardware conflicts, so they're usually caused by the
expansion cards in your computer.  One of the most likely boards to encounter
this is a serial card with more than 2 ports.  If the card supports shared
IRQs, then set it to another IRQ.  If not, then each port has it's own IRQ.
In this case, disable all but one port and test that.  Repeat the test on each
port until you find the port that is causing the conflict and then set that
port to another IRQ.

The situation that is referred to above as "slow processing" is where a window
appears locked up or even a locked desktop (mouse, buttons, cursors, etc.) can
be detected with a benchmark program that monitors cpu usage such as memsize,
pmpatrol, etc.  If the cpu usage is moving, then the programs are still
running, and so is the operating system.  You'll need to check all programs in
the tasklist after it recovers and check each by itself to see if it reproduces
the problem.
